course: everyday
lesson: 218
title: Balconies, blades, batteries and strange bottles
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: Even something small dropped from a balcony falls fast and can badly hurt a person below, so nothing is ever thrown or dropped from a height.
explain: Sharp things are carried and passed safely: child scissors closed and pointing down, handle first; grown-ups' razors and box cutters are not for children, and a used needle found outside is never touched.
explain: A button battery swallowed by a small child can burn through the throat within about two hours, so it means going to hospital at once.
explain: Medicines are medicine, not candy, and an unknown liquid in an old drink bottle may be a chemical; you never drink it.
explain: A boy in Brisbane hears his toddler sister go quiet in the next room; he checks and finds her opening the remote control, and takes the battery away.
fact: Never throw or drop anything from a balcony, because a falling object can seriously hurt someone below.
fact: When clearing the table, carry a few plates at a time, scrape leftovers into the bin or food caddy and stack them by the sink.
fact: Carry scissors closed with the points down, and pass them handle first.
fact: Children never use or play with grown-ups' razors, because the blades are extremely sharp.
fact: A grown-up opens parcels with a craft knife or box cutter, because the blades slip easily.
fact: A used needle or syringe found outside is never touched, because it can carry dangerous infections; you tell a grown-up.
fact: A quiet toddler is often getting into something dangerous, so you go and check.
fact: A swallowed button battery means going to hospital immediately.
fact: Strong magnets are kept away from bank cards and electronics.
fact: Medicine should be called medicine, never candy or sweets.
fact: People sometimes keep chemicals in old drink bottles, so you never drink from a bottle you are not sure of.
fact: Children's medicines have special doses, so you ask a grown-up before taking any.
exercise: You are on a balcony on the tenth floor with a small ball. What do you do? | Keep it on the balcony and never drop it | Drop it over the edge to see how high it bounces back | Throw it to a friend down below | Drop it only if nobody is looking up
exercise: Why is dropping even a small thing from a balcony dangerous? | It falls fast and can seriously hurt someone below | Small things float down slowly | It only matters for heavy things | People below can always catch it
exercise: You clear the table after dinner. What is the safest way? | Carry a few plates at a time and stack them by the sink | Carry the whole pile at once to save time | Run to the sink with the plates | Leave the leftovers on the plates and carry them in one tall stack
exercise: You need to pass scissors to a friend. How? | Closed, handle first | Open, with the points first | Thrown gently across the table | Open, so she can use them at once
exercise: You find your dad's razor by the bathroom sink. What do you do? | Leave it alone | Try shaving your arm to see how it works | Play with it carefully | Take the blade out to look at it
exercise: A parcel arrives and you want to open it with a box cutter. What should you do? | Ask a grown-up to open it | Open it fast before anyone sees | Cut towards yourself to be careful | Use the box cutter with a friend holding the box
exercise: You find a syringe with a needle in the park. What do you do? | Do not touch it and tell a grown-up | Pick it up carefully and put it in the nearest bin | Kick it under a bush | Take it home to show your parents
exercise: Why should you never touch a used needle? | It can carry dangerous infections | It might be worth money | The plastic might melt | Needles are only for grown-ups to collect
exercise: Your little sister is suddenly very quiet in the next room. What do you do? | Go and check what she is doing | Enjoy the peace and keep playing | Wait until she starts crying loudly | Close the door so she stays there
exercise: A button battery falls out of a remote near the baby. What should happen? | Keep it away from little children and dispose of it safely | Put it back on the floor for later | Give it to the baby to hold | Leave it in a toy box
exercise: A toddler may have swallowed a button battery. What do you do? | Go to hospital immediately | Wait and see if he feels ill | Give him a drink and forget it | Wait for it to come out naturally
exercise: Where should strong magnets be kept? | Away from bank cards and electronics | On top of the laptop | Next to the bank cards in a wallet, so you can find them | Inside a phone case
exercise: A babysitter tells your toddler brother his medicine is candy. Why is that a problem? | He may look for more "candy" and take medicine | Candy is healthier than medicine, so he'll want it more | Medicine stops working if called candy | He will not want any real candy
exercise: In the garage there is a lemonade bottle with a strange liquid in it. What do you do? | Never drink from it | Take a small sip to check | Smell it closely to find out | Drink it if it is a nice colour
exercise: You have a headache and see adult painkillers in the cupboard. What do you do? | Ask a grown-up for help | Take one, since you are nearly grown up | Take half an adult tablet | Take two if the headache is bad
test: Kids on a high balcony want to drop a water balloon onto the street. What is true? | It could seriously hurt someone below | Water balloons are always harmless, they just splash | It is fine if they shout first | It will slow down before landing
test: A girl in Belfast carries six plates and a bowl at once and drops them. What would have worked better? | Carrying a few plates at a time | Carrying them faster | Holding them with one hand | Asking someone to open the door
test: Which is the safe way to walk across a classroom with scissors? | Closed, with the points down | Open, pointing forward so people see them | Spinning them on a finger | Points up, near your face
test: A boy in Florida finds a needle on the beach. What should he do? | Leave it and tell an adult at once | Bury it in the sand | Throw it into the sea | Pick it up with his fingers carefully
test: Why is it dangerous if a small child swallows a button battery? | It can burn through the throat within about two hours | It makes the child feel full | It passes through and does no harm | It only causes a small tummy ache
test: Your cousin has a box of very strong magnets. Which of these should be kept well away from them? | Bank cards | Paper photos | Wooden spoons | Plastic cups
test: A drink bottle on a garden shed shelf is half full of blue liquid. What is the safest thought? | It might be a chemical, so leave it | It is probably just a sports drink someone forgot | It is fine if it smells sweet | Pour it into a cup and taste it
test: Why should children not take adult medicine on their own? | Children's doses are different and adult medicine can harm them | Adult medicine never works on children | Medicine always tastes bad for children | Children are not ill often enough
test: Why is it wiser to call medicine "medicine" around little children? | So they do not think it is a treat and look for more | So they learn a long word | Because candy is more expensive | So it tastes better to them
test: clearin up, can i carry all 10 plates at once | Carry a few at a time | Yes, stack them up high | Yes, if you run really fast | Carry them on your head
test: parcel came, gonna open it with dads craft knife | Let a grown-up open it, the blade slips | Yes, cut towards you so you can see it | Yes, use it quickly | Use dad's razor instead
test: my bank card was next to the fridge magnets and dont work now, coincidence? | Strong magnets can wipe the strips on bank cards | Magnets have no effect on cards | Cards break in the cold | The fridge is too loud
test: theres a sports bottle in the garage with clear stuff, its water right? | Don't drink it, people keep chemicals in old bottles | Yes, clear means water | Smell it and sip | Drink it if you're thirsty
explain: A toddler in New York climbed onto a chair by an open window; she fell from the second floor and was badly hurt, which is why window guards are now law in many flats there.
explain: A little boy swallowed a button battery from a remote control; within two hours it burned a hole in his throat, and he needed many operations.
fact: Falls from windows and balconies badly injure many young children every year, often when furniture is pushed near them.
fact: A swallowed button battery can burn through the throat in about two hours, so it is always an emergency.
test: baby's playing with a tiny battery from the remote | Take it away and tell an adult right now | Let him, it's not charged so it can't do anything | Wait and see if he swallows it | Give him a toy instead and leave it
test: Why should chairs never be pushed near an open window or a balcony rail? | Small children climb them and can fall | The chairs get wet when it rains through the window | It blocks the view | Chairs wear out the floor
test: found an old drink bottle in the shed with blue stuff in it | Don't drink or sniff it, tell an adult | Taste a drop to check | Pour it on the plants | Take it to school
test: did u hear a toddler swallowed one of those little round batteries and it burnt a hole in her throat? real lesson? | A swallowed button battery is a hospital emergency at once | Wait to see if it comes out by itself | Give the child milk and let them sleep | Only big batteries are dangerous
test: apparently a kid dropped a water balloon off the 10th floor balcony and it knocked a man out cold. should u try it from the 2nd floor? | No, never drop anything from a balcony | Yes, the 2nd floor is low enough to be totally safe | Yes, if you shout a warning first | Yes, water balloons are soft
test: my cousin said her friend drank from an old lemonade bottle in the shed and it was weedkiller and she nearly died. real lesson? | Never drink from a bottle you're not sure of | Only drink lemonade from the shed if it's still fizzy | Smell it first to check | Old bottles always hold water
exam: Identify the proper manner in which scissors are carried and passed. | Closed, points downward, and passed handle first | Open, points forward, and passed to the other blade first | Thrown gently to the recipient | Carried open in a pocket
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises a swallowed button battery? | It may burn through the throat within about two hours and is an emergency | It passes harmlessly in nearly all cases | It is dangerous only if bitten | It requires attention only the next day
exam: Select the response consistent with established practice upon finding a used needle outside. | It is not touched, and an adult is informed | It is collected with care and placed in the nearest bin | It is pushed aside with a shoe | It is carried home to show a parent
exam: Ascertain the principal reason for which medicine is never described to children as sweets. | Children may consume it believing it to be sweets | Medicine tastes considerably better when it is so described | It is a description required by law | Medicine and sweets contain the same ingredients
